procedures.canonicalJson:
canonicalJson(value) -> text. Input is a JSON value (null, boolean, finite number, string, array, object with string keys).
  null -> "null"; true -> "true"; false -> "false".
  number -> the ECMAScript Number-to-String text, as JSON.stringify writes it:
    - an integer-valued number has no fraction and no exponent while its magnitude is below 1e21: 1, -5, 100, 9007199254740991, 123456789012345680000;
    - negative zero is "0";
    - otherwise the shortest decimal text that reads back to the same IEEE 754 double: 0.1, 1.5, -2.25, 5e-324;
    - magnitude 1e21 or more uses an exponent with a plus sign: "1e+21"; magnitude below 1e-6 uses "1e-7", "1.5e-7";
    - never ".0", never a leading "+", lower-case "e".
  string -> a double quote, then each UTF-16 code unit in order, then a double quote, with these escapes only:
    - U+0022 -> \" and U+005C -> \\;
    - U+0008 -> \b, U+0009 -> \t, U+000A -> \n, U+000C -> \f, U+000D -> \r;
    - every other code unit below U+0020 -> \u00XX with lower-case hex digits;
    - a surrogate code unit that is not part of a valid pair -> \uXXXX with lower-case hex digits;
    - everything else is written as itself: "/" is not escaped, U+007F, U+2028, U+2029 and all non-ASCII characters are written literally.
  array -> "[" + items joined by "," + "]"; no spaces.
  object -> "{" + members joined by "," + "}", each member canonicalJson(key) + ":" + canonicalJson(member value);
    members are ordered by key, comparing keys as sequences of UTF-16 code units (so "Z" < "a", and U+FF5E sorts after a character outside the Basic Multilingual Plane, whose first code unit is at most U+DBFF).
A value that is not JSON (undefined, a function) is an error. NaN and the infinities would be written "null"; they cannot arrive over JSON and must not be produced.
The text is turned into bytes as UTF-8 wherever it is hashed or signed.

procedures.sdpFingerprint:
fingerprint(description) -> text or none:
  split description.sdp into lines at LF or CRLF;
  keep lines that start with "a=fingerprint:sha-256 " (letters compared without case);
  for each, drop the first 14 characters ("a=fingerprint:"), trim white space, lower-case the rest;
  remove duplicates; the result is valid only when exactly one distinct value remains and it matches ^sha-256 (?:[a-f0-9]{2}:){31}[a-f0-9]{2}$; otherwise none.
binding(peer) = { local: fingerprint(local description of the connection to peer), remote: fingerprint(remote description) }, available only while the "reploid" data channel to that peer is open and both fingerprints are valid.
Orientation rule: the challenger puts its own binding in the challenge (local = challenger's certificate, remote = the other side's). The side that answers accepts the challenge only when challenge.local equals ITS remote and challenge.remote equals ITS local. A proof is therefore valid only on the DTLS connection it was made for.

procedures.identityChallengeAnswer:
On reploid:identity-challenge from connection P (answer at any time, including in the middle of a request):
  ignore the frame unless ALL hold: binding(P) exists; challenge.roomId == own room id; challenge.requester == P; challenge.provider == own connection name;
    challenge.nonce matches ^[a-f0-9-]{36}$; challenge.local == binding(P).remote; challenge.remote == binding(P).local;
  ignore a second challenge with the same (P, nonce) while the first is being signed;
  signature = sign(own private key, identityProofSignedBytes(challenge, own participantId));
  if binding(P) is unchanged, send reploid:identity-proof { nonce: challenge.nonce, participantId: own participantId, publicJwk: own public key, signature: base64url(signature) }.
No error frame is sent for a refused challenge; the challenger times out.

procedures.generationTerminalVerification:
On reploid:generation-error with a terminal member, for a pending request whose provider identity was proven:
  canonical = the object with exactly the members objects.generationTerminal.x-signedPayloadFields taken from terminal (malformed terminal -> ignore it);
  require peerIdFromPublicJwk(terminal.publicJwk) == canonical.provider;
  require canonical.requestId == requestId, provider == proven provider identity, consumer == own participantId, model == requested model id, modelIdentity == requested modelIdentity, adapterIdentities equal, inputDigest == hashJson(messages as sent), and no settingsDigest;
  require verify(terminal.publicJwk, base64url-decode(terminal.signature), UTF-8(canonicalJson(canonical))).
If all hold, the terminal is evidence that the provider ended the request with status "failed" or "cancelled" and cleaned up. If any fails, ignore the terminal. Either way the request ends with the error text.
